WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.500
Now, as part of sick day management, it's important to actually get a sick day

2
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.070
management kit ready. And what that kit looks like depends on the

3
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.900
person, but wherever you're working, you can actually help them build that kit.

4
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:24.060
So here's one I have prepared earlier.

5
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.440
And what we'll do is we'll go through all the different components in there

6
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.341
together. So the number one thing to have in your kit is,

7
00:00:32.381 --> 00:00:36.140
of course, your sick day management plan, and making sure that that's up to date,

8
00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.980
that that's reviewed regularly, because it might not be the person using it, it

9
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.740
might be their loved ones or someone else.

10
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.820
Also, talking to the person about having a list of emergency

11
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.740
contacts, so their local GP, their local pharmacy, and their

12
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.660
loved ones in case they do get sick really quickly, and having that all together is

13
00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.540
great. Now, monitoring, of course, is essential for

14
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:02.250
someone when they do get sick. And so whether or not that's glucose or ketones,

15
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.020
depending on the type of diabetes and what recommendations you've made for them.

16
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:11.000
And so having an in-date BGL monitor and making sure that the

17
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.260
strips are in date because they do go out of date.

18
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.800
And depending on what sort of diabetes, they might want to have a spare

19
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.140
continuous glucose monitor in there.

20
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.360
And again, making sure that those products are up to date.

21
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.300
Then if somebody is using an insulin pump, having some

22
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.330
spare bits and pieces to go with their pump, just having that

23
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.060
in the same spot as well, and making sure that they're in

24
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.060
date too. So the other thing is having somewhere

25
00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.040
to write down their blood glucose monitoring or their ketone monitoring,

26
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:50.200
because not all people living with diabetes will regularly

27
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:54.140
test their blood glucose levels. Particularly someone, for example,

28
00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.230
living with type 2, maybe they're just on metformin, they're pretty

29
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.500
well-controlled, so they might not actually have

30
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.580
somewhere to record. So just having a really simple

31
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:09.100
record book in there, or even just a pen and paper. Doesn't matter.

32
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.240
Whatever works for the person there.

33
00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.269
And of course, having hypo treatments is key, and

34
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.140
what hypo treatments are in there depends on the person

35
00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.260
and their preference. So looking at your regular jelly beans

36
00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.280
or your lollies of preference.

37
00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.020
And of course, depending on the type of diabetes, the age of the person, you

38
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.320
might want to consider having a glucagon pen in there, too,

39
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.160
and making sure that family members or whoever the person is living with

40
00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:42.580
is aware that those products are in there and also aware of how to use them

41
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:43.880
and how many to use.

42
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.000
Having some sort of carbohydrate in there as well,

43
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.960
particularly if someone has got vomiting, nausea,

44
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.080
diarrhea, and they're not eating well.

45
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.980
So having just something like some dry biscuits in there that's

46
00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.370
not going to go out of date again

47
00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.880
quickly, and making sure that the person

48
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.140
knows just to keep them in there. They're not snacks for the rest of the house.

49
00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.540
They're just essential for the kit, for the person.

50
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.720
And then, of course, the all-important hydration management.

51
00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.299
So something as simple as having a drink bottle in there.

52
00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.420
Not all individuals are great at carrying around drink bottles.

53
00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.130
And when you're feeling sick and you can't find your drink bottle because you've

54
00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.040
left it at work or those sorts of issues.

55
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:31.420
So just letting the person know a great idea is just to keep a drink bottle

56
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.020
in there. And then, of course, you've got your oral rehydration

57
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.900
solution. Now, it's also important to have on-hand because

58
00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:42.780
it is more effective at hydrating quicker than water

59
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.690
alone. And so sometimes having a selection of products, a selection of

60
00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.549
oral rehydration solution in a sick day kit is great, because I don't know about

61
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:51.030
you, but

62
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.540
when I'm sick, sometimes my taste buds go and I don't always feel like a certain

63
00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.000
flavor or a certain type.

64
00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:01.950
So Hydralyte has quite a few different products in their range that they can

65
00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.680
use. So you've got your ready-made solution.

66
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:08.580
Those sorts of things are great, particularly when the person is feeling really

67
00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:12.460
awful and they're not wanting to measure 100

68
00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.780
mils or measure 200 mils, and this is just simple, easy,

69
00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.601
and ready to go. Depending on

70
00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:23.660
storage and that sort of thing, they might want to consider something smaller like

71
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.000
the dissolvable tablets or even the sachets.

72
00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:31.190
There's actually a few products out there that have multi-flavors as

73
00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:34.980
well. So having different flavors for different times is a great

74
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:35.500
idea.

75
00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:39.180
And the other really important one is

76
00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:43.920
icy poles. Now, particularly in kids, I find

77
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:47.530
that icy poles are great, just because kids are really prone to

78
00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:51.040
guzzling water. If they're vomiting and diarrhea, they guzzle too quickly, they're

79
00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.400
just going to vomit it straight out.

80
00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:55.820
So when you freeze a product and it's in an icy pole

81
00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.780
version, then it's slower to ingest and also less

82
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.200
likely to come back out. So never discount

83
00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:07.140
the icy poles as well. And the last product that I

84
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.140
think is really key to have in a sick day kit, because it's great, like

85
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.080
I said, to have everything there, is to have some sort of pain management

86
00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:19.120
as well. It's also great as health professionals to talk about

87
00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:23.140
what sort of pain management that that person might be safe to have,

88
00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.120
particularly when you consider anti-inflammatories and other drugs might impact

89
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.620
on kidneys, and particularly when someone is

90
00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:34.490
dehydrated. So looking at your non-anti-inflammatory options is key

91
00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.940
there. And of course, taking those

92
00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:41.880
opportunistic times when you do see people to ask them not only do

93
00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.680
they have their sick day management plan, is it up to date, and do they have their

94
00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:48.360
kit ready to go at home in the off chance that they are sick?
